body:
With `{before: true}`, callers suppressed inside the same debounce window can resolve with `undefined` while the leading invocation is still pending. The leading call later resolves with its actual value.

The timer reads `leadingValue` before the async leading branch has assigned it. That means the successful same-window callers no longer share the leading result, unlike the existing `before option - all calls in window resolve to same leading value` test.

Reproduced on current main `792115ea04019c720ddc8753010c758c55796f77`, Node 22.23.3. The leading function below stays pending behind an explicit resolver; there is no dependency on an external service.

Save as `repro-leading.test.js` in the repo and run `node --test repro-leading.test.js`:

```js
import test from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import {setTimeout as delay} from 'node:timers/promises';
import pDebounce from './index.js';

test('slow leading call keeps suppressed callers pending for the same result', async () => {
  let finish;
  let calls = 0;
  const debounced = pDebounce(() => {
    calls++;
    return new Promise(resolve => { finish = resolve; });
  }, 10, {before: true});
  const first = debounced();
  let secondSettled = false;
  const second = debounced().then(value => {
    secondSettled = true;
    return value;
  });
  await delay(40);
  const settledBeforeResult = secondSettled;
  finish(42);
  const results = await Promise.all([first, second]);
  console.log({settledBeforeResult, results, calls});
  assert.equal(settledBeforeResult, false);
  assert.deepEqual(results, [42, 42]);
  assert.equal(calls, 1);
});
```

Actual: `{settledBeforeResult: true, results: [42, undefined], calls: 1}`. The first assertion fails.

Expected: both promises stay pending until the leading invocation completes, then resolve to 42, with only one invocation in this window.

I also ran the existing `fn takes longer than wait` and `before option - all calls in window resolve to same leading value` tests; both pass, but the former is trailing mode and the latter resolves immediately, so neither covers this combination. This is separate from the abort-listener cleanup problem in #34.

Suggested direction: retain the leading promise per debounce batch and have suppressed callers adopt that promise, rather than reading a value which may not exist yet. Keep it batch-local so a later leading window cannot replace the result for an earlier one.
